# /etc/logrotate.d/rsyslog on the WALL PANEL -- installed by wall-firstboot.sh
# on every boot in place of the distribution's file of the same name (SR-083).
#
# WHY IT TAKES THAT NAME RATHER THAN ADDING A FILE BESIDE IT. logrotate refuses
# a log named by two stanzas ("duplicate log entry") and skips it, so a second
# file listing /var/log/syslog would have disabled rotation of syslog
# altogether. The package's own file is not overwritten: firstboot DIVERTS it
# (dpkg-divert --local --rename) to /etc/logrotate.rsyslog.distrib, outside the
# include directory, so rsyslog upgrades write there with no conffile prompt.
# Undo: rm this file, then dpkg-divert --rename --remove /etc/logrotate.d/rsyslog.
# The list of files and the postrotate below are the stock Ubuntu 24.04 ones,
# unchanged; only the schedule and the size cap differ. When rsyslog updates,
# compare the .distrib file with this one.
#
# WHY. On 2026-10-18 at 00:02 the weekly rotation gzipped a week of syslog that
# had grown to tens of GB (the compressed result alone was 944 MB) and filled
# the root filesystem for about two minutes; the kiosk's cookie store stopped
# persisting and the next restart lost the Pandora sign-in. Nothing capped
# rsyslog's file. journald holds the same messages under its own explicit cap
# (wall-journald.conf), so this copy only needs to be short.
#
# THE CAP: daily, or sooner once a file passes 100 MB, seven generations kept.
# `maxsize` is only checked when logrotate RUNS, and the stock logrotate.timer
# is daily -- so wall-logrotate-hourly.conf re-schedules that same timer to run
# hourly (AccuracySec=5min, so up to ~65 minutes between runs).
#
# THE REAL BOUND, per log file, for a sustained write rate R bytes/hour and a
# gzip ratio c (syslog text is typically 10x or better):
#   G = 100 MB + R x 65 min      the most one generation can hold, because the
#                                size is only looked at once per run
#   live file        <= G
#   .1 (delaycompress, still uncompressed until the NEXT rotation) <= G
#   .2.gz .. .7.gz   <= 6 x G / c
#   while compressing .1: a temporary up to G / c beside it
# so about 2G + 7G/c. At the 2026-10-18 rate (tens of GB a week, call it
# 200 MB/h) G is about 320 MB and the syslog total about 0.9 GB; at 1 GB/h it is
# about 2.7 GB + 1.5 GB. The six files are capped independently, but on this
# panel syslog carries nearly everything (kern/auth/user/cron/mail are small
# subsets), so the syslog figure is the budget. The panel's root is 98 GB.
# The low-disk alarm (wall-disk-check) is the backstop for a rate beyond that.
# logrotate.service's stock ConditionACPower=true is left alone: the panel
# runs on mains power.
/var/log/syslog
/var/log/mail.log
/var/log/kern.log
/var/log/auth.log
/var/log/user.log
/var/log/cron.log
{
	daily
	maxsize 100M
	rotate 7
	missingok
	notifempty
	compress
	delaycompress
	sharedscripts
	postrotate
		/usr/lib/rsyslog/rsyslog-rotate
	endscript
}
